    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p51-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Expand $(k^4-6k^2+1)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k^8-12k^6+38k^4-12k^2+1$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}(k^4-6k^2+1)^2&=k^8+36k^4+1-12k^6+2k^4-12k^2\\\\&=k^8-12k^6+38k^4-12k^2+1.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, or otherwise, prove that for all integers $k>1$, the solutions of the equation\n$$x^2-(k^4+2k^2+1)x+(4k^6-8k^4+4k^2)=0$$\nyield the squares of the lengths of the two legs of this triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [
          {
            "id": "p51-q18-b-diagram",
            "role": "question",
            "format": "tikz",
            "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 51 p51-q18-b-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,2.4);\n\\coordinate (B) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (3.7,0);\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;\n\\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,0.28)--(0.28,0.28)--(0.28,0);\n\\path (A)--(C) node[midway,above,sloped,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,text=black] {$k^2+1$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
            "src": null,
            "widthMm": 48,
            "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
            "overlayOf": null,
            "transparent": false,
            "axes": null,
            "spec": {
              "sourcePage": 51,
              "sourceRegion": "Question 18b, triangle beneath the equation",
              "description": "Unshaded right triangle with vertical left leg, horizontal bottom leg, descending hypotenuse and a lower-left right-angle square. Coordinates determine illustrative proportions only; no numerical leg lengths are supplied.",
              "mathematics": "The hypotenuse is labelled k^2+1. Both legs are unlabelled and perpendicular.",
              "answerVisibility": "The right-angle marker and hypotenuse label are permanent student givens; no solution leg labels are shown."
            },
            "derived": false,
            "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
          }
        ],
        "children": [],
        "dependsOn": [
          "p51-q18-a"
        ],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The roots are $(k^2-1)^2$ and $(2k)^2$. Since $(k^2-1)^2+(2k)^2=(k^2+1)^2$ and both leg lengths are positive for $k>1$, these are the squares of the two leg lengths.",
          "worked": "Here $a=1$, $b=-(k^4+2k^2+1)$ and $c=4k^6-8k^4+4k^2$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\Delta&=(k^4+2k^2+1)^2-4(4k^6-8k^4+4k^2)\\\\&=k^8-12k^6+38k^4-12k^2+1\\\\&=(k^4-6k^2+1)^2.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe last equality uses part a. By the quadratic formula,\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{k^4+2k^2+1\\pm\\sqrt{(k^4-6k^2+1)^2}}{2}\\\\&=\\frac{k^4+2k^2+1\\pm|k^4-6k^2+1|}{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe two signs give the same unordered pair if the absolute value is replaced by $k^4-6k^2+1$. Thus\n$$\\begin{aligned}x_1&=\\frac{k^4+2k^2+1+(k^4-6k^2+1)}{2}\\\\&=k^4-2k^2+1\\\\&=(k^2-1)^2,\\\\x_2&=\\frac{k^4+2k^2+1-(k^4-6k^2+1)}{2}\\\\&=4k^2\\\\&=(2k)^2.\\end{aligned}$$\nFor integers $k>1$, the lengths $k^2-1$ and $2k$ are positive. Also,\n$$\\begin{aligned}(k^2-1)^2+(2k)^2&=k^4+2k^2+1\\\\&=(k^2+1)^2.\\end{aligned}$$\nBy Pythagoras’ theorem, the roots are the squares of the two leg lengths of a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse $k^2+1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      }
    ]
  },
